# Šank — local development.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && npm run dev
#
# That is the whole setup. The first boot creates data/sank.db, runs the
# migrations, installs the triggers from server/database/triggers.sql and seeds
# the venue "Lounge" with six people, 27 tables and a menu.
#
# Nothing in this file is a secret and nothing in it is safe on a server. The
# real values live in /opt/sank/.env on the VPS and nowhere else — see
# deploy/deploy.env.example for that list and deploy/README-DEPLOY.md for what
# each one does.

# --- the three that make a dev machine a dev machine ------------------------

# Turns on POST /api/dev/enrol, which mints a device cookie without an enrol
# code so you can get a session with one curl (or one button, once WP9 lands):
#
#   curl -c jar.txt -X POST localhost:3100/api/dev/enrol \
#        -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{}'
#   curl -b jar.txt -c jar.txt -X POST localhost:3100/api/auth/pin \
#        -H 'content-type: application/json' \
#        -d '{"user_id":"<Amar's id from the response above>","pin":"1111"}'
#
# Both bodies are `.strict()`, so `-d '{}'` is required and not decoration: a
# POST with no body at all is a 400 INVALID_BODY, not an empty object.
#
# It also turns on POST /api/dev/reset-limits, which forgets every rate-limit
# window. The Playwright suite calls it once per spec file: nine files from one
# IP would otherwise exhaust authLimiter's ten calls a minute and the last four
# would never run. The leash is not widened for tests — this is a door beside it.
#
# Both routes 404 outright unless this is exactly "1". /opt/sank/.env never sets
# it: it would hand an enrolled device cookie to the internet.
SANK_DEV_ENROL=1

# Session and device cookies are `Secure` by default, which means a browser
# throws them away over plain http://localhost. This is the only thing that
# turns that off, and it is off *only* here (BACKEND §5.4).
COOKIE_SECURE=0

# The pepper is mixed into every PIN hash before scrypt, so a stolen database
# file is not a list of guessable four-digit PINs. In production it is 32 random
# bytes (`openssl rand -hex 32`); here it is the word "dev", because a seeded
# PIN hashed on your laptop should still verify on your laptop tomorrow.
#
# Changing it invalidates every PIN already stored. On a dev database that just
# means deleting data/sank.db and letting it reseed.
PIN_PEPPER=dev

# --- optional ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the database file lives, relative to the project root. The default.
# DB_PATH=data/sank.db

# Hourly copies of the database into <dir>/hourly and a daily one into
# <dir>/daily, taken with SQLite's online backup API by server/tasks/backup.ts.
# The task is skipped entirely when this is unset — which is how the VPS is
# configured, because there cron owns backups instead (a cron job survives a
# wedged Node process; a task inside it does not).
# BACKUP_DIR=data/backups

# The address the app answers on as the outside world sees it. Šank sends no
# notifications of any kind, so nothing here is a delivery setting: the Dnevnik
# and the attention list inside the app are the only channels.
# PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3100

# X-Forwarded-For and X-Real-IP are ignored unless this is "1", because
# otherwise every rate-limit bucket is chosen by whoever sends the header. It is
# "1" on the VPS and only there, because only there is nginx overwriting both
# (see deploy/nginx.conf).
# TRUST_PROXY=0

# Where uploaded photos are written: `<UPLOAD_DIR>/{chat,delivery}/YYYY/MM/<uuid>.jpg`,
# mode 0600. `data/` is already gitignored, so the default keeps a dev machine's
# photos out of the repo without another rule. On the VPS this is
# /var/lib/sank/uploads — see deploy/deploy.env.example.
# UPLOAD_DIR=data/uploads

# *Prijem sa slike* reads an otpremnica with the Anthropic API, server-side only
# (server/services/scanModel.ts is the only file that imports the SDK, and the
# key never reaches a phone). **Optional**: with it unset the route answers
# 503 SCAN_NOT_CONFIGURED and the typed *Ručno* form is right there, which is a
# supported state, not a broken one. CI never has it.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# Installs the deterministic stub model instead — a fixed eight-line otpremnica,
# six green, one amber, one unknown. Read once at boot, never true on the VPS,
# and the reason the Phase 4 verification run costs nothing and never calls the
# API. Exactly "1" or it does nothing.
# SANK_SCAN_STUB=1
